{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And if his servant's tooth.</b> The reason the verse did not include tooth and eye together and divided them into two commandments may be explained according to what they said, of blessed memory, in Kiddushin (Kiddushin 24b): Rabbi Sheshet said: if his eye was dim and he gouged it out — he goes free. According to this, there are two laws of emancipation regarding the eye: the first — if his eye was dim, even though he did not gouge it out, as is understood there in Kiddushin, that even though it was not gouged out, whenever he diminished its light and he cannot use it — he goes free. And the second — that even if it was dim, if he gouged it out — he goes free. And had the verse included them in one law, we would have learned the eye according to the law of the tooth: just as the tooth — its nullification is its loss, so too the eye — its nullification is its loss; but dimming that does not diminish the eye at all — he would not go free."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:06.029532216Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}